<!-- Default locale = English: automatic fallback for unsupported device languages.

     L10n rule of the fake-editor design, three registers (PLANNING Fase 18). The
     REGISTER decides the language, not the punctuation around it:

       CODE  -> always English: JSON keys, filenames, job ids, rule variables and
                operators, cron fields, $ commands, git chrome (commit, Author:, @@,
                hashes), verdicts (pass / unstable / fail), levels (ERROR:, WARN:),
                net::ERR_* codes, licenses, URLs, one-word markers (# stale) and the
                file banners (// Tweather Configuration File).
       DATA  -> localized: weather values, city names, day names, moon phases.
       PROSE -> localized wherever it appears: README.md, HELP.md, first run,
                notifications, accessibility text, AND the comment lines that are
                sentences (// tap to enable, // hint: ..., the sky.crontab notes).

     Two tests, in order: would translating it break a lookup, a filename, a
     copy-paste or the alignment with a key printed elsewhere (then it is code)?
     would git translate it (git translates "nothing to commit, working tree clean"
     and never "commit")? One line may hold both registers: keep the tokens and
     translate around them.

     This replaces the old wording ("// comments stay English"), which mistook the
     channel for the register.

     Implemented in Fase 18. The screens take a `Resources` and put the marker in
     front of a resource; the two builders that are pure values and mean to stay
     that way — `SkyDocumentBuilder` and `WidgetContentBuilder` — take their
     sentences in as strings instead, with an English fallback that `SkyNotesTest`
     and `WidgetNotesTest` tie to this file word for word. `RegisterRuleTest`
     checks every `note_*` at once: none carries its own marker or level, and every
     one of them says something different in Italian. -->
